---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/compare/tinyemail-vs-shopify-email";
const title = "TinyEmail vs Shopify Email: choosing Shopify store email in 2026";
const desc = "Shopify Email is now part of Shopify Messaging. Compare the native workflow with tinyEmail\u2019s Shopify app without treating either as a compulsory stepping stone.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Shopify Email lack automation?",
    "answer": "No. Shopify Messaging documents action-triggered marketing automations and supports custom workflows through Shopify Flow. Match the exact trigger and recipient eligibility to your use case."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I run both recovery flows during migration?",
    "answer": "Prepare the replacement disabled and inspect its behavior first. Assign one live sender to each recovery trigger before enabling it so that the same customer does not receive competing series."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p></header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Both offer store automation</h2>
      <p>tinyEmail advertises cart, welcome and winback email with synced store data. Shopify documents action-triggered marketing automations and custom workflows through Shopify Flow. The old claim that native email is limited to a single basic reminder is not a sound reason to install another app. Start with the campaign or automation that your current setup cannot comfortably deliver.</p>
      <h2>Native administration versus another app</h2>
      <p>Staying in Shopify Messaging keeps campaign work close to store administration. tinyEmail introduces a separate editor and customer/product/order sync, which can be worthwhile if the team prefers its workflow. Rehearse updating a collection announcement and a welcome message in each candidate. Inspect the mobile preview, product destinations and reusable brand elements; do not infer better design or faster work from a template count.</p>
      <h2>Recovery requires precise trigger ownership</h2>
      <p>Distinguish abandoned cart from abandoned checkout, and identify which native automations are already active. In a proposed rehearsal, complete an order after entering recovery and inspect whether pending reminders remain appropriate. Check eligibility separately from template appearance. When adding tinyEmail, designate one live recovery owner and disable the displaced automation before activating its replacement; parallel live sends are not a migration safeguard.</p>
      <h2>Free offers have different boundaries</h2>
      <p>Shopify documents 10,000 included emails per calendar month on Basic, Grow, Advanced and Plus, followed by usage charges; marketing automation emails count toward the allowance. tinyEmail advertises a free Shopify offer with unlimited contacts and a monthly send allowance stated in its FAQ. Compare expected campaign recipients and automation sends against each offer. Neither the old tinyEmail paid-contact estimates nor the phrase zero marginal cost describes every store.</p>
      <h2>Channel needs deserve their own decision</h2>
      <p>Shopify Messaging documentation covers email and other messaging channels; availability and charges are channel specific. An email comparison does not establish SMS eligibility or a complete cross-channel stack. If text is required, specify destination countries, opt-in sources and the actual campaign type before choosing. Do not assume that a channel in a product menu makes every store eligible.</p>
      <h2>Choose for the work you will maintain</h2>
      <p>Keep native email when the team can maintain its campaigns and automations without a recurring problem. Evaluate tinyEmail when its app workflow or offer resolves a specific constraint. Before switching, map consent, forms, recovery ownership and product links, then inspect a small set of records and disabled messages. There is no need to migrate at an arbitrary number of monthly orders, or to claim that a longer trial guarantees stronger revenue.</p>
      <h2>Primary sources</h2>
      <p>Product information checked November 25, 2026. <a class="link" href="https://www.tinyemail.com/shopify-email-marketing">tinyEmail Shopify features</a> · <a class="link" href="https://www.tinyemail.com/faqs">tinyEmail allowance</a> · <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/email/pricing/">Shopify email pricing</a> · <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations">Shopify automations</a></p>
    </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/tinyemail-vs-mailchimp">TinyEmail vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-shopify-email">Klaviyo vs Shopify Email</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/jewelry">Email apps for jewelry stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/welcome-series">Welcome series playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions about this comparison" />
</BaseLayout>
